  essays=[
    ('2.7','Taxing disposable packaging','sl','shops distribute cheap single-use packaging, while litter collection imposes costs on the council.','Explain how a specific indirect tax changes competitive market outcomes.','evaluate whether an indirect tax is the most effective response to excessive disposable packaging.','Supply shifts up by the tax; consumer price rises, net producer price falls and traded quantity falls. Explain the wedge, tax revenue and the role of relative elasticities.','A tax can change relative prices and fund collection, but effectiveness depends on substitutes and responsiveness. Low-income users may lose relatively more; information, deposit-return systems or standards could complement the tax. Judge by damage reduction and administrative feasibility, not revenue alone.','tax'),
    ('2.7','Rent controls and housing access','sl','the city proposes a below-equilibrium rent ceiling, but construction capacity is constrained.','Explain why a binding rent ceiling may cause a shortage and non-price rationing.','discuss whether rent ceilings improve housing affordability for low-income households.','Explain contracted supply and extended demand at the controlled rent; distinguish shortage from an inward shift. A correctly labelled diagram shows Qs below Qd. Queues and landlord selection replace some price rationing.','Incumbent tenants can gain while new entrants face queues and quality loss. Long-run investment, enforcement, eligibility and public provision matter. Compare targeted housing support and supply measures, allowing justified support for a temporary ceiling with complementary provision.','demand-up'),
    ('2.8','Managing a shared fishery','sl','many boats can access a coastal fishery and each catch leaves fewer fish for others.','Explain why open access may lead to overuse of a fishery.','evaluate alternative policies for managing common access resources sustainably.','Fish stocks are rival and access is difficult to exclude. Individual boats consider private returns but not the stock damage imposed on others and future users. Market effort can exceed a socially efficient sustainable level.','Compare quotas/licences, protected areas, community rights and taxes. Monitoring, scientific uncertainty, livelihoods and cross-border spillovers affect success. A coherent policy package may outperform an isolated measure; a supported different ranking merits credit.','externality'),
    ('2.9','Providing flood-warning services','sl','a regional warning system protects all residents in range and exclusion is difficult.','Explain why a public good may be underprovided by a private market.','evaluate whether government financing is sufficient to ensure effective provision of public goods.','Explain non-rivalry and non-excludability, incentives to free ride and the difficulty of capturing willingness to pay as private revenue. Distinguish the public service from rival physical equipment.','Tax finance can overcome voluntary underpayment but allocation, procurement and accountability still matter. Compare direct state production with contracting and explain opportunity cost, information and maintenance. A reasoned judgement distinguishes financing from effective delivery.',None),
    ('2.11','Competition in a concentrated industry','ahl','a few large battery manufacturers face expensive research and plant entry requirements.','Explain why oligopolistic firms are interdependent and may compete through product development.','discuss whether strong competition policy always improves outcomes in concentrated markets.','Each firm anticipates rivals\' price and innovation responses. Explain barriers to entry and non-price competition, avoiding an assumption that concentration proves collusion.','Competition policy can constrain collusion, abuse and exclusion. Large scale may reduce average costs and finance research, while market power may weaken incentives and raise prices. Compare contestability, dynamic efficiency and enforcement costs before a conditional judgement.',None),
    ('2.4','Consumer nudges and rational choice','ahl','a pension plan switches from opt-in to automatic enrolment with a free opt-out.','Explain how bounded rationality and choice architecture can affect consumer decisions.','evaluate whether nudges are preferable to financial incentives for changing consumer behaviour.','Explain attention limits, inertia and framing. A default can alter behaviour without removing alternatives, while distinguishing bounded rationality from a claim that consumers never optimize.','Nudges may be inexpensive and preserve choice but may fail when affordability or strong preferences dominate. Incentives change costs and can have distributional effects. Compare transparency, heterogeneous preferences and persistence, and justify which combination suits the objective.',None)
  ]
  for f,e in enumerate(essays):
    for v in range(3):
      code,title,level,scenario,a,b,am,bm,d=e
      essay(topic,f'micro-essay-{f+1:02}',v,code,title+' — '+CASES[(f*3+v)%26],level,'In '+CASES[(f*3+v)%26]+', '+scenario,a,b,am,bm,'ceiling' if f==1 else d)

  data=[
    ('2.3','Refill markets after a festival', [('demand',['Quantities buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices.','In a specified period, other relevant influences unchanged.']),('competitive equilibrium',['The price/output combination at which market quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.','There is no excess demand or supply at that price.'])],
     'A festival raises visitor numbers and demand for refill bottles. Later, a resin shortage raises producers\' input costs. Officials consider supporting refill infrastructure; small sellers have limited finance.',
     [('Using a diagram, explain the festival\'s effect on equilibrium bottle price and quantity.',['Label price/quantity axes, S and D1/D2.','Shift D right with higher price and quantity.','More visitors raise demand at each price.','At the old price a shortage raises price until the market clears.']),
      ('Using a diagram, explain how the resin shortage affects the bottle market.',['Label D, S1/S2 and the price/quantity axes.','Shift supply left, raising price and reducing quantity.','Higher resin costs lower profitability at each selling price.','The supply shock reduces traded output, other things equal.']),
      ('Explain why the total effect on equilibrium quantity is uncertain when both changes occur.',['Demand shifts right.','Supply shifts left.','The demand change raises quantity but the supply change lowers it.','The net quantity effect depends on relative magnitudes, although both put upward pressure on price.']),
      ('Explain how investment in refill infrastructure could affect productive capacity.',['New equipment is capital investment.','It can increase the number/efficiency of refill points.','Lower unit costs can shift supply right over time.','Finance, maintenance and adoption determine whether potential capacity becomes actual output.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate whether public support for refill infrastructure is likely to improve market outcomes.','Higher visitor demand creates a capacity opportunity but resin costs and finance constrain supply. Infrastructure may lower costs and reduce waste, but output gains depend on utilization and access for small sellers. Compare grants, information and simpler entry requirements; consider opportunity cost, distribution and supplier capture. The controlled PED evidence informs price responsiveness but does not identify the festival demand shift.','demand-up'),
    ('2.6','A perishable fruit market',[('price elasticity of supply',['Responsiveness of quantity supplied to changes in price.','PES equals the percentage quantity-supplied change divided by the percentage price change.']),('opportunity cost',['Value of the next best alternative forgone.','It arises because scarce resources have competing uses.'])],
     'A storm destroys part of the fruit harvest; unchilled fruit spoils quickly. Growers can add trees only over several seasons. The council considers shared cold storage, while rural households rely heavily on farm income.',
     [('Using a diagram, explain the effect of the storm on the fruit market.',['Label D, S1/S2 and price/quantity.','Show supply shifting left, raising price and lowering quantity.','Harvest destruction reduces available supply at each price.','With inelastic demand, price may rise sharply; its size depends on elasticities.']),
      ('Explain why the short-run PES may be low.',['Current harvest is constrained by existing trees.','Fruit takes time to grow.','A higher price cannot rapidly increase mature trees or recover destroyed fruit.','Perishability and storage constraints further limit timing of sales.']),
      ('Using a demand diagram, explain why a fall in household income may reduce demand for premium fruit.',['Label price/quantity axes and D1/D2.','For a normal good shift D left.','Lower income reduces willingness and ability to buy at each price.','This is a demand shift, not a movement caused by the fruit\'s own price.']),
      ('Explain how cold storage could affect supply responsiveness.',['Storage allows some fruit to be carried between periods.','Growers can adjust the timing of sales.','This may make supply more responsive over the relevant time horizon.','Electricity, capacity and spoilage costs limit the improvement.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the provision of shared cold storage as a response to unstable fruit prices.','Storage can reduce spoilage and smooth supply, with benefits for farm income and consumers. It cannot instantly replace a destroyed crop or alter biological growing times. Consider demand elasticity, electricity reliability, access rules and the risk that large growers capture subsidies. Compare insurance, diversification or infrastructure and give a justified conditional judgement.','supply-down'),
    ('2.8','Urban journeys and air quality',[('negative externality of consumption',['A cost imposed on third parties by consumption.','It is not fully included in the consumer\'s private cost-benefit decision.']),('subsidy',['A payment by government to support production or consumption.','A per-unit payment reduces the effective cost or raises the return on each unit.'])],
     'Private car journeys create local air pollution and delays for other road users. The city plans a bus fare subsidy; buses have spare seats outside the peak but are full during rush hour. Low-income shift workers travel when service is infrequent.',
     [('Using an externality diagram, explain why car use may exceed its socially efficient level.',['Label MPB above MSB and MSC=MPC, with quantity and costs/benefits axes.','Show market quantity above efficient quantity.','Drivers do not fully account for third-party pollution/delay costs.','For excess journeys MSC exceeds MSB, producing welfare loss.']),
      ('Using a supply and demand diagram, explain the effect of a bus subsidy.',['Label D, S and subsidized S, with price/quantity axes.','Shift effective supply down/right; consumer fare falls and journeys rise.','The subsidy lowers the price needed to cover each bus journey.','The size of the expansion depends on available capacity and demand responsiveness.']),
      ('Explain why a lower bus fare may reduce demand for car journeys.',['Bus and car journeys can be substitutes.','A lower bus fare lowers the relative cost of bus travel.','Some consumers switch, shifting demand for car journeys left.','The effect may be small where bus frequency, routes or travel time are unsuitable.']),
      ('Explain one reason a fare subsidy may have a different effect during peak and off-peak periods.',['Peak buses are already full.','Off-peak buses have spare seats.','A lower fare alone cannot quickly increase peak capacity.','Off-peak demand can be accommodated more readily, so quantity and crowding effects differ.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate whether a bus fare subsidy is an effective response to external costs of car use.','Lower fares can encourage substitution and benefit low-income users, but full peak buses and sparse shift-worker services weaken access. Combine fare support with targeted service capacity or road-use pricing; compare fiscal cost, environmental gain and equity. Use the controlled elasticity study as one piece of evidence, not proof that every car user will switch.','positive'),
    ('2.8','Learning access and wider benefits',[('positive externality of consumption',['A benefit accruing to third parties because of consumption.','The benefit is not fully included in the consumer\'s private incentive.']),('equity',['A judgement about fairness in distribution or treatment.','It can support different allocations according to needs or circumstances, rather than necessarily identical outcomes.'])],
     'Adult literacy courses improve learners\' opportunities and help them support children\'s learning. Providers charge fees, and low-income workers face childcare and travel barriers. The council proposes fee subsidies, but qualified teachers are scarce.',
     [('Using an externality diagram, explain why literacy courses may be underconsumed.',['Label MSB above MPB and MSC=MPC.','Show efficient quantity above private market quantity.','Learners do not capture every benefit to children and others.','The omitted benefits make some socially worthwhile courses privately unattractive.']),
      ('Using a supply and demand diagram, explain the intended effect of a course subsidy.',['Label course price/quantity, D, S and S after subsidy.','Show lower learner price and higher enrolment.','The subsidy reduces effective supply cost.','Expansion depends on teacher availability and learners\' response.']),
      ('Explain how childcare support may influence course demand.',['Childcare is a complementary input to attendance for some learners.','Lower childcare costs reduce the overall cost of taking a course.','Demand for courses can shift right at each tuition fee.','The effect is largest among people whose binding barrier was childcare.']),
      ('Explain why teacher scarcity may limit the short-run effect on enrolment.',['Training qualified teachers takes time.','Current supply is relatively price inelastic near capacity.','Extra demand/subsidies may then raise provider receipts more than course places.','Longer-run training and facilities can relax the constraint.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the effectiveness of fee subsidies in improving equitable access to adult literacy.','Subsidies address monetary fees and external benefits, but teacher scarcity and non-fee barriers can dominate. Compare targeted subsidies with childcare, flexible hours and teacher training, considering cost, targeting error and long-run benefits. Reach a conditional judgement grounded in the stated constraints.','positive'),
    ('2.8','Waste disposal and shared resources',[('common access resource',['A resource whose use is rival.','Excluding users is difficult or costly.']),('market failure',['A market allocation that fails to maximize social welfare.','Private incentives do not lead to the socially efficient quantity or allocation.'])],
     'Households dump household waste beside a shared wetland when collection is inconvenient. Wildlife habitat and nearby water quality decline. The municipality considers convenient collection points funded by a household charge; remote villages have poor road access.',
     [('Using a negative-consumption-externality diagram, explain excessive damaging disposal.',['Label MPB above MSB and MSC=MPC for the damaging disposal activity.','Show private quantity above efficient quantity.','Households ignore some ecological and water-quality costs to others.','At excess disposal units social cost exceeds social benefit.']),
      ('Explain why the wetland can be overused as a waste sink.',['Its environmental absorption capacity is finite and rival.','Exclusion/monitoring is difficult.','Each dumper obtains a private convenience benefit while sharing damage with many people.','This creates incentives to overuse a common resource.']),
      ('Using a demand diagram, explain how convenient collection can reduce demand for informal dumping.',['Label the price/generalized cost and quantity of informal dumping, D1 and D2.','Shift dumping demand left.','Convenient formal collection is a closer substitute.','At a given private dumping cost more households choose collection.']),
      ('Explain why a uniform household charge can raise equity concerns.',['Equal charges need not represent equal shares of income.','Lower-income households may bear a larger proportional burden.','Remote villages may receive worse service despite paying the same charge.','A targeted concession or differentiated access can address one concern, but requires information and funding.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate a collection-point programme financed by a household charge.','Better access can substitute away from dumping and protect the wetland, but poor roads and uneven coverage may limit effects. A flat charge can discourage participation or burden poor households. Consider tax financing, targeted concessions, enforcement and education; weigh monitoring cost and sustainability with a supported conditional conclusion.','externality'),
    ('2.9','Clean-water monitoring',[('public good',['A good or service that is non-rival in consumption.','It is non-excludable, so non-payers cannot readily be prevented from benefiting.']),('free rider',['Someone who benefits without contributing toward cost.','Non-excludability can create an incentive not to reveal willingness to pay.'])],
     'A public water-quality alert is broadcast to every household in range. Private water filters are sold separately. Some upstream firms pollute, and poorer residents cannot easily buy filters. The authority considers public monitoring and enforceable discharge standards.',
     [('Explain why the broadcast alert is a public good but a household filter is not.',['One person hearing an alert does not reduce another\'s benefit.','Non-paying residents cannot readily be excluded from the broadcast.','A physical filter is rival in use/capacity.','Sellers can exclude buyers who do not pay for a filter.']),
      ('Using a production-externality diagram, explain the pollution problem.',['Label MSC above MPC, MSB=MPB and both axes.','Show market output above efficient output.','Firms omit damage to water users from private costs.','Reducing excess polluting output or emissions can recover welfare.']),
      ('Using a demand diagram, explain how higher household income could affect filter demand if filters are normal goods.',['Label filter price/quantity and D1/D2.','Shift D right.','A normal good\'s demand rises with income at each price.','It is a shift due to income rather than a movement due to filter price.']),
      ('Explain one challenge in enforcing discharge standards.',['The authority needs reliable information about emissions.','Firms may conceal or shift discharge times.','Monitoring and penalties must make compliance credible.','Costs and weak administrative capacity can reduce the effectiveness of the legal rule.'])],
     'Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate public monitoring and discharge standards as a response to unsafe water.','Alerts overcome a public-good financing problem but do not remove pollution. Standards address the source and can protect people unable to buy filters, yet require credible monitoring and affordable compliance. Compare taxes, treatment infrastructure and household support; prioritize prevention or a combination on stated evidence.','externality')
  ]
  for f,(code,title,defs,text,analyses,g,gm,d) in enumerate(data):
    for v in range(3):
      p0=20+5*v;p1=p0+5;q0=100+20*v;q1=q0-(10+5*v)
      stem=f'All places, observations and values below are constructed for practice. Case {CASES[f*3+v]}: '+text+'\n\nA separate controlled demand study holds all non-price influences constant. It is not a before/after estimate of the policies in the text.\n\n'+table(['Study observation','Price (crowns/unit)','Quantity demanded (units/week)'],[['Initial',p0,q0],['New',p1,q1]])+'Show numerical working. Give exact values or round to two decimal places, and retain relevant units.'
      ps,nums=drparts(defs,p0,p1,q0,q1,analyses,g,gm)
      if f in [2,4]:d='externality-cons'
      add(topic,f'micro-data-{f+1:02}',v,code,title+' — '+CASES[f*3+v],title,'sl','paper-2','data-response',stem,ps,d,nums,'hard')

  for f in range(6):
    for v in range(3):
      town=CASES[8+f*3+v if 8+f*3+v<26 else v];base=40+4*v
      if f in [0,1,2,4]:
        # Inverse demand P=A-Q; inverse private supply P=B+Q. Exact linear model.
        A=100+10*v;B=20+2*v;Q=Fraction(A-B,2);P=Fraction(A+B,2);w=12+4*v
        action=['a per-unit tax','a per-unit subsidy','a tax matching constant marginal external cost','a maximum price'][[0,1,2,4].index(f)]
        stem=f'This is a fictional policy case in {town}. Inverse demand is P={A}−Q and inverse private supply is P={B}+Q; P is crowns per unit and Q is units per day. Curves are linear over the range used. '
        if f==0:
          code='2.7';title='Tax incidence and a packaging policy';qn=Q-w/2;pc=P+w/2;pp=P-w/2;stem+=f'The government considers a specific tax of {w} crowns per unit on disposable packages. Demand is relatively unresponsive for small food vendors; refill substitutes require an initial equipment purchase.';diagram='tax'
          subs=[('a(i)','Calculate the untaxed equilibrium price and quantity.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q.'),('A1',f'Q={fmt(Q)} units/day.'),('M1',f'P={B}+{fmt(Q)}.'),('A1',f'P={fmt(P)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(ii)','Calculate the taxed quantity, consumer price and producer net price.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q+{w}.'),('A1',f'Q={fmt(qn)} units/day.'),('A1',f'Consumer price={fmt(pc)} crowns/unit.'),('A1',f'Producer net price={fmt(pp)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(iii)','Calculate daily government tax revenue and explain why revenue is not itself a net welfare gain.',3,[('M1',f'{w}×{fmt(qn)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(w*qn)} crowns/day.'),('R1','Tax revenue transfers purchasing power to government; it is not newly created resources, so other stakeholder effects must also be counted.')]),('a(iv)','Calculate the daily loss of total surplus if there are no externalities and explain why tax revenue is excluded from that loss.',3,[('M1',f'1/2×{w}×({fmt(Q)}−{fmt(qn)}).'),('A1',f'{fmt(w*(Q-qn)/2)} crowns/day.'),('R1','It is the triangle of mutually beneficial trades prevented; tax revenue is a transfer, not itself a social loss.')]),('a(v)','Explain how a tax can still improve welfare when packaging has external costs.',6,[('A1','Unpriced disposal damage creates a gap between MSC and MPC.'),('R1','The private equilibrium can then involve excessive packaging.'),('A1','A tax raises the private marginal cost faced by decision makers.'),('R1','It can reduce output toward MSB=MSC.'),('A1','A correctly sized tax reflects marginal external damage at the efficient output.'),('R1','The no-externality loss in part (iv) is therefore not automatically the net welfare effect in the actual case.')])]
          prompt='Recommend a policy or policy package to reduce disposable packaging while considering the constraints faced by small vendors.';model='Use the calculated reduction in sales and tax receipts. A phased tax with recycling/refill support can alter incentives while helping vendors finance substitutes. Weigh monitoring, regressivity and uncertain external costs; an alternative combination of deposit-return and standards is acceptable if its mechanism and suitability are justified.'
        elif f==1:
          code='2.7';title='Insulation subsidies and fiscal cost';qn=Q+w/2;pc=P-w/2;pp=P+w/2;stem+=f'A subsidy of {w} crowns per unit is considered for insulation. Some residents rent their homes and cannot authorize installation. Qualified installers are scarce.';diagram='subsidy'
          subs=[('a(i)','Calculate the unsubsidized equilibrium.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q.'),('A1',f'Q={fmt(Q)} units/day.'),('M1',f'P={B}+{fmt(Q)}.'),('A1',f'P={fmt(P)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(ii)','Calculate subsidized output, consumer price and producer receipt.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q+{w}={B}+Q.'),('A1',f'Q={fmt(qn)} units/day.'),('A1',f'Buyer price={fmt(pc)} crowns/unit.'),('A1',f'Producer receipt={fmt(pp)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(iii)','Calculate total daily subsidy spending and state why spending alone does not establish a welfare benefit.',3,[('M1',f'{w}×{fmt(qn)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(w*qn)} crowns/day.'),('R1','The payment uses public resources; expenditure alone is not the net social benefit, which requires comparison with benefits, distortions and opportunity cost.')]),('a(iv)','Calculate the daily increase in buyer expenditure net of the government payment (new minus old).',3,[('M1',f'New expenditure={fmt(pc)}×{fmt(qn)}.'),('M1',f'Old expenditure={fmt(P)}×{fmt(Q)}.'),('A1',f'Change={fmt(pc*qn-P*Q)} crowns/day; distinguish spending from welfare.')]),('a(v)','Explain two reasons installation may expand less than the simple model predicts.',6,[('A1','Installers may already operate near capacity.'),('R1','Short-run supply is then less elastic than the fitted model.'),('R1','More support may raise receipts with a smaller quantity response.'),('A1','Renters face a decision/benefit split with landlords.'),('R1','A lower equipment price may not overcome lack of authorization.'),('R1','Complementary training or landlord incentives can address these distinct barriers.')])]
          prompt='Recommend an approach to improve access to home insulation within a limited public budget.';model='Use the calculated buyer price, uptake and fiscal spending. Targeted subsidy plus installer training or landlord conditions may outperform a universal payment, but eligibility checks cost money and training takes time. Compare immediate affordability with medium-term capacity and justify a conditional recommendation rather than treating subsidy expenditure as a welfare benefit.'
        elif f==2:
          code='2.8';title='Pollution damage and efficient output';qn=Q-w/2;pc=P+w/2;pp=P-w/2;stem+=f'Production creates constant marginal external damage of {w} crowns per unit. MSB equals private demand; MSC equals private supply plus that damage. Monitoring output is easy, but actual emissions differ across firms.';diagram='externality'
          subs=[('a(i)','Calculate private market output and price.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q.'),('A1',f'Qm={fmt(Q)} units/day.'),('M1',f'P={B}+{fmt(Q)}.'),('A1',f'P={fmt(P)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(ii)','Calculate the socially efficient output and associated MSB.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q+{w}.'),('A1',f'Q*={fmt(qn)} units/day.'),('M1',f'MSB={A}−{fmt(qn)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(pc)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(iii)','Calculate the daily welfare loss of the unregulated market and explain why the stated external-cost wedge is used.',3,[('M1',f'1/2×({fmt(Q)}−{fmt(qn)})×{w}.'),('A1',f'{fmt((Q-qn)*w/2)} crowns/day.'),('R1','At Qm, MSC−MSB equals the external-cost wedge; the gap narrows linearly to zero at Q*.')]),('a(iv)','Calculate revenue from a tax equal to marginal external damage.',3,[('M1',f'Tax={w} crowns/unit and taxed output={fmt(qn)} units/day.'),('M1',f'Revenue={w}×{fmt(qn)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(w*qn)} crowns/day.')]),('a(v)','Explain why an emissions charge may be preferable to an output tax for heterogeneous firms.',6,[('A1','An output tax treats units alike even when emissions per unit differ.'),('R1','It may not reward cleaner technology sufficiently.'),('A1','An emissions charge attaches a cost to measured damage-generating activity.'),('R1','Firms can respond by reducing emissions intensity as well as output.'),('A1','Accurate emissions measurement is required.'),('R1','Higher monitoring cost or weak enforcement may favour a simpler policy in this context.')])]
          prompt='Recommend a policy to address pollution, drawing on the efficient-output calculation and the monitoring constraints.';model='A corrective output tax matches the given constant damage model and reduces output toward Q*. Yet varying emissions intensities strengthen the case for a monitored emissions charge or standards. Weigh enforcement cost, cleaner technology incentives and distribution, using the calculated welfare loss as a model estimate rather than an observed empirical gain.'
        else:
          code='2.7';title='A price ceiling and targeted access';ceiling=P-w/2;qs=ceiling-B;qd=A-ceiling;stem+=f'A legal maximum price is set at {fmt(ceiling)} crowns. The good is a staple. The authority cannot guarantee every household receives a ration; some retailers cut opening hours.';diagram='ceiling'
          subs=[('a(i)','Calculate the unrestricted equilibrium.',4,[('M1',f'{A}−Q={B}+Q.'),('A1',f'Q={fmt(Q)} units/day.'),('M1',f'P={B}+{fmt(Q)}.'),('A1',f'P={fmt(P)} crowns/unit.')]),('a(ii)','Calculate quantities supplied and demanded at the ceiling.',4,[('M1',f'Qs={fmt(ceiling)}−{B}.'),('A1',f'Qs={fmt(qs)} units/day.'),('M1',f'Qd={A}−{fmt(ceiling)}.'),('A1',f'Qd={fmt(qd)} units/day.')]),('a(iii)','Calculate the shortage and legal market sales revenue, assuming all supplied units sell.',4,[('M1',f'Shortage={fmt(qd)}−{fmt(qs)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(qd-qs)} units/day.'),('M1',f'Revenue={fmt(ceiling)}×{fmt(qs)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(ceiling*qs)} crowns/day.')]),('a(iv)','Calculate the government cost of buying the shortage abroad at the ceiling price, if this were feasible.',2,[('M1',f'{fmt(qd-qs)}×{fmt(ceiling)}.'),('A1',f'{fmt((qd-qs)*ceiling)} crowns/day; this assumes sufficient external supply at the stated price.')]),('a(v)','Explain why a lower legal price need not improve access for every poor household.',6,[('A1','Some households receive cheaper units.'),('R1','But the quantity available falls in the stated model.'),('A1','Queues and search costs ration the shortage.'),('R1','People with inflexible work hours may be excluded.'),('A1','Retail opening-hour reductions further impair access.'),('R1','Ability to pay is only one barrier; allocation rules and supply matter.')])]
          prompt='Recommend an approach to improve staple access for low-income households, using the shortage and cost evidence.';model='Compare targeted transfers/vouchers, public supply and a temporary ceiling. The calculated shortage warns that a ceiling alone cannot ensure access; targeted support can preserve supply incentives but may raise prices if supply is rigid. Public procurement needs credible finance and logistics. A combined, conditional approach or another justified priority earns credit.'
        nums=[{'expression':f'({A}-{B})/2','expected':float(Q)},{'expression':f'({A}+{B})/2','expected':float(P)}]
        if f!=4:nums+=[{'expression':f'({A}-{B}{"+" if f==1 else "-"}{w})/2','expected':float(qn)}]
      else:
        code='2.11';diagram=None;price=30+4*v; costs=[0,16+v,30+2*v,48+3*v,76+4*v,112+5*v];price_list=[price+8,price+4,price,price-4,price-8];trs=[(q+1)*price_list[q] for q in range(5)];profits=[trs[q]-costs[q+1] for q in range(5)];best=profits.index(max(profits))+1
        title='Monopoly pricing and access' if f==3 else 'Scale, costs and producer objectives'
        stem=f'All values are constructed. A sole supplier in {town} chooses only among the listed daily quantities; fixed cost is zero in this simplified schedule.\n\n'+table(['Q (units/day)','Price (crowns/unit)','Total cost (crowns/day)'],[[q,price_list[q-1],costs[q]] for q in range(1,6)])+('The product is an essential diagnostic kit. Entry is difficult because of licensing and research costs; the regulator is considering a price rule.' if f==3 else 'The firm is a recycling processor. It can pursue profit or wider collection coverage. A smaller rival could enter if collection contracts are opened to bidding.')
        subs=[('a(i)','Calculate total revenue and profit at Q=3.',4,[('M1',f'TR=3×{price_list[2]}.'),('A1',f'{trs[2]} crowns/day.'),('M1',f'Profit={trs[2]}−{costs[3]}.'),('A1',f'{profits[2]} crowns/day.')]),('a(ii)','Calculate profit at each other listed quantity and identify the profit-maximizing choice.',5,[('A1',f'Q1 profit={profits[0]} crowns/day.'),('A1',f'Q2 profit={profits[1]} crowns/day.'),('A1',f'Q4 profit={profits[3]} crowns/day.'),('A1',f'Q5 profit={profits[4]} crowns/day.'),('A1',f'Choose Q={best} units/day for maximum listed profit {max(profits)} crowns/day.')]),('a(iii)','Calculate marginal revenue and marginal cost for the move from Q=3 to Q=4.',4,[('M1',f'MR={trs[3]}−{trs[2]}.'),('A1',f'{trs[3]-trs[2]} crowns/unit.'),('M1',f'MC={costs[4]}−{costs[3]}.'),('A1',f'{costs[4]-costs[3]} crowns/unit.')]),('a(iv)','Calculate average total cost at Q=3, and explain its distinction from marginal cost.',3,[('M1',f'AC={costs[3]}/3.'),('A1',f'{fmt(Fraction(costs[3],3))} crowns/unit.'),('R1','AC spreads total cost across all three units; MC is the cost of the additional unit.')]),('a(v)','Explain two reasons the profit-maximizing output may differ from the socially preferred output.',4,[('A1','Market power makes price exceed marginal revenue.'),('R1','Profit maximization need not give price equal to marginal social cost.'),('A1','Access, external benefits/costs or equity may also matter to society.'),('R1','A socially preferred output needs information on these effects, not just maximum revenue or profit.')])]
        prompt='Recommend a policy to improve outcomes in this market, using the cost and revenue evidence.'
        model='Use the maximum-profit choice and marginal comparisons rather than assuming the highest output is efficient. Price regulation can improve access but may weaken cost recovery and innovation. Competitive procurement/entry can reduce market power if entry is credible; public support needs safeguards. Explain the trade-off between access, quality, finance and incentives and make the recommendation contingent on actual costs and barriers.'
        nums=[{'expression':f'3*{price_list[2]}-{costs[3]}','expected':profits[2]},{'expression':f'{trs[3]}-{trs[2]}','expected':trs[3]-trs[2]},{'expression':f'{costs[4]}-{costs[3]}','expected':costs[4]-costs[3]}]
      if f==5:
        # A distinct scale/plant comparison, not another copy of the discrete
        # monopoly-output table used in family 4.
        code='2.11';title='Plant scale, unit costs and competition';diagram=None
        oldq=100+20*v;newq=2*oldq;oldtc=1500+250*v;newtc=2600+450*v;price=20+v
        oldac=Fraction(oldtc,oldq);newac=Fraction(newtc,newq);oldprofit=price*oldq-oldtc;newprofit=price*newq-newtc;incmc=Fraction(newtc-oldtc,newq-oldq)
        stem=f'This fictional case concerns a recycling processor in {town}. At its current plant scale, daily output is {oldq} units and total daily cost is {oldtc} crowns. A larger plant could produce {newq} units at a total daily cost of {newtc} crowns. The selling price is assumed constant at {price} crowns/unit in this project comparison, and all output can be sold. These are alternative fully adjusted plant scales, not a short-run variable-labour experiment. Larger processing capacity could lower collection charges, but an exclusive collection contract may prevent rival entry.'
        subs=[('a(i)','Calculate current average total cost and profit.',4,[('M1',f'AC={oldtc}/{oldq}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(oldac)} crowns/unit.'),('M1',f'Profit={price}×{oldq}−{oldtc}.'),('A1',f'{oldprofit} crowns/day.')]),
          ('a(ii)','Calculate average total cost and profit at the larger plant.',4,[('M1',f'AC={newtc}/{newq}.'),('A1',f'{fmt(newac)} crowns/unit.'),('M1',f'Profit={price}×{newq}−{newtc}.'),('A1',f'{newprofit} crowns/day.')]),
          ('a(iii)','Calculate the percentage change in average cost and the change in daily sales revenue.',4,[('M1',f'Percentage change=({newac}−{oldac})/({oldac})×100.'),('A1',f'{fmt((newac-oldac)/oldac*100)}%.'),('M1',f'Revenue change={price}×({newq}−{oldq}).'),('A1',f'{price*(newq-oldq)} crowns/day.')]),
          ('a(iv)','Calculate the additional cost per extra unit between the plant choices and explain its interpretation.',4,[('M1',f'Incremental cost=({newtc}−{oldtc})/({newq}−{oldq}).'),('A1',f'{fmt(incmc)} crowns per extra unit.'),('R1','This is average incremental cost across a discrete capacity change.'),('R1','It is not proof of the exact marginal cost at every intervening quantity.')]),
          ('a(v)','Explain why these data can indicate economies of scale without contradicting diminishing marginal returns.',4,[('A1','Average total cost is lower at the larger fully adjusted plant scale.'),('R1','Specialization or spreading fixed setup costs can produce economies of scale.'),('A1','Diminishing marginal returns is a short-run argument with at least one fixed input.'),('R1','The plant comparison changes scale in the long run, so the two concepts concern different conditions.')])]
        prompt='Recommend an approach to recycling-processing expansion that considers unit costs, access and competition.'
        model='Use the lower average cost and increased projected profit to explain scale benefits, while recognizing the assumptions of constant price and full sales. Competitive tendering or regulated access may share savings with users and avoid an exclusive contract entrenching market power. Compare contract quality, capacity demand and monitoring costs; expansion alone does not guarantee lower consumer charges.'
        nums=[{'expression':f'{oldtc}/{oldq}','expected':float(oldac)},{'expression':f'{newtc}/{newq}','expected':float(newac)},{'expression':f'{price}*{newq}-{newtc}','expected':newprofit}]
      ps=[point(label,prompt_,marks,*steps) for label,prompt_,marks,steps in subs]
      ps.append(bandpart('b',prompt,10,'policy',prompt,model))
      stem+='\n\nThis complete Paper 3 style case awards 20 marks for part (a) and 10 for the policy recommendation. Show working; give exact answers or round to two decimal places.'
      add(topic,f'micro-policy-{f+1:02}',v,code,title+' — '+town,title,'ahl','paper-3','policy-response',stem,ps,diagram,nums,'hard')
